// clock_now — SELF-HOSTED `datetime.now()` + `env.millis()` over the WASI wall-clock
// floor (prim.clock_time_get), the same template as env_unix_timestamp (clock_id 0 =
// CLOCK_REALTIME, precision 0, nanoseconds read from an 8-ALIGNED window inside a 16-byte
// scratch Bytes dropped at scope end — wasmtime's clock_time_get TRAPS on an unaligned
// timestamp pointer, and `handle+12` is only 4-aligned). `datetime.now` returns Unix SECONDS (native `as_secs()`), `env.millis`
// MILLISECONDS (native `as_millis() as i64`). Both bodies reach prim.clock_time_get, so
// their cap_witness carries Capability::Clock. The PUBLIC surface declares every one of
// these `effect fn` (#1515 — a wall-clock read is nondeterminism, E006-gated like the
// entropy surface); bundled-stdlib effect fns are never Result-lifted, so callers still
// see the raw Int. scripts/check-cap-effect-consistency.sh holds the rule.
// NON-DETERMINISTIC (reads the wall clock): no byte-match across runs.
fn datetime_now() -> Int = {
  let buf = prim.alloc_bytes(16)
  let p = int.band(prim.handle(buf) + 12 + 7, 0 - 8)
  let _e = prim.clock_time_get(0, 0, p)
  let ns = prim.load64(p)
  ns / 1000000000
}

fn env_millis() -> Int = {
  let buf = prim.alloc_bytes(16)
  let p = int.band(prim.handle(buf) + 12 + 7, 0 - 8)
  let _e = prim.clock_time_get(0, 0, p)
  let ns = prim.load64(p)
  ns / 1000000
}

// `datetime.monotonic_ns` — the same scratch/alignment template, but clock_id 1
// = CLOCK_MONOTONIC and NO division: the return is raw NANOSECONDS. This was the
// one datetime fn with no wasm body, so any file touching it fell off the wasm
// leg entirely (the #1189 ratchet's COMPILER_FRONTIER class).
// The ORIGIN differs by target and is allowed to: native counts from the first
// call (OnceLock<Instant>), WASI from its own unspecified epoch. That is exactly
// the documented contract — "nanoseconds since an unspecified reference point;
// only DIFFERENCES are meaningful; never goes backwards" — so the promise both
// targets keep is monotonicity and the ns unit, never the absolute value.
// NON-DETERMINISTIC (reads a clock): no byte-match across runs or targets.
fn datetime_monotonic_ns() -> Int = {
  let buf = prim.alloc_bytes(16)
  let p = int.band(prim.handle(buf) + 12 + 7, 0 - 8)
  let _e = prim.clock_time_get(1, 0, p)
  prim.load64(p)
}
